@*
    The feasible region of a two-ingredient ration, drawn flat. This is the one figure on the site
    that shows what the solver is actually doing rather than what it returned, so it is worth being
    exact about: the geometry below is a real linear program, not a sketch that resembles one.

    Let x = maize %, y = soybean meal %. Three constraints:

        2.1x +      y >=  72.5      a minimum  (steep line, left edge)
        6x   +  17y   <= 490        a maximum  (shallow line, upper edge)      [y <= 28.82 - 0.353x]
       -0.375x +   y  >= -1.75      a minimum  (rising line, lower-right edge)

    Those three bound the triangle A(25, 20) - B(42, 14) - C(30, 9.5).

    Cost = 1.725x + 2y, so the iso-cost lines have slope -0.8625 and soybean meal is dearer per
    point than maize, which is true of the real ingredient library. That objective is chosen so its
    gradient lies inside the normal cone at C -- it is the positive combination 1*(2.1, 1) +
    1*(-0.375, 1) of the two constraints active there -- which makes C the unique minimiser:

        cost(A) = 83.13     cost(B) = 100.45     cost(C) = 70.75   <-- the optimum

    C is therefore a vertex of the feasible set, and the drawn iso-cost line through it is tangent
    to the region. Both of those are the whole theorem the app rests on, and both are things an
    examiner can check off the picture.

    Screen mapping: x in 0..70 across px 44..464, y in 0..35 across py 314..14 (inverted). The
    viewBox is a window onto that space, cropped just outside the ticks and the two arrow tips.

    No numerals on the axes. This is a schematic -- putting "20" on an axis invites "20% of what,
    measured how?", a question the figure cannot answer honestly. Tick marks give it the structure
    of a chart without claiming to be data.

    There is no type inside the drawing at all: every word belongs to the page. See the note on the
    axis titles below for why.
*@

<figure class="fc-lp">
    @* Axis titles are markup rather than <text> in the plot, for the same reason the key below is.
       font-size inside an SVG resolves in user units, so it scales with the viewBox: one value that
       sets 15px on a desktop renders 10px where this figure is 327px wide and 21px where a stacked
       column makes it 672px, and neither is the size an axis title should be. Out here they set once,
       in the page's own type, at a size that holds. Nothing is lost by moving them -- an axis title
       only has to sit on the right side of the plot, it is not pinned to a tick. *@
    <div class="fc-lp-frame">
        <span class="fc-lp-axlabel fc-lp-axlabel-y">Soybean meal %</span>

        <svg class="fc-lp-plot" viewBox="32 0 448 330" role="img" aria-labelledby="lpTitle lpDesc">
            <title id="lpTitle">The feasible region of a two-ingredient ration</title>
            <desc id="lpDesc">
                A graph with maize percentage across the bottom and soybean meal percentage up the
                side. Three straight lines, each one a nutrient requirement, enclose a shaded
                triangle containing every mix that satisfies all of them. A family of parallel dashed
                lines runs across the graph joining mixes of equal cost, and an arrow across them
                shows cost falling towards the bottom left. The last dashed line to touch the
                triangle meets it at a single corner, marked with a diamond: that corner is the
                cheapest mix, and it is what the solver returns.
            </desc>

            @* Cost contours first, so the constraints and the region sit over them. *@
            <g class="fc-lp-iso">
                <line x1="44" y1="176.9" x2="155.3" y2="314" />
                <line x1="44" y1="121.1" x2="200.5" y2="314" />
                <line x1="44" y1="65.4" x2="245.7" y2="314" />
                @* The tangent contour: the cheapest one that still touches the region, through C. *@
                <line x1="46.6" y1="14" x2="290.1" y2="314" />
            </g>

            @* Which way cost falls. Perpendicular to the contours as they appear on screen rather
               than in ration percentages: the axes are at different scales -- 6 px per maize point
               against 8.57 per soybean point -- so the true gradient of 1.725x + 2y would sit 20
               degrees off a right angle here and read as a slip. The direction is correct under
               either reading; only the angle is a drawing decision. *@
            <g class="fc-lp-arrow">
                <line x1="132" y1="186" x2="104" y2="209" />
                <path d="M 96 215.5 L 100.88 205.69 L 106.58 212.65 Z" />
            </g>

            @* The three requirements. Drawn full width so each reads as a constraint on the whole
               plane rather than an edge that only exists where the triangle needs it. *@
            <g class="fc-lp-constraint">
                <line x1="151.1" y1="14" x2="251.1" y2="314" />
                <line x1="44" y1="66.9" x2="464" y2="278.7" />
                <line x1="72" y1="314" x2="464" y2="104" />
            </g>

            @* A - B - C. Every edge lies on one of the three lines above, by construction. *@
            <path class="fc-lp-region" d="M 194 142.6 L 296 194 L 224 232.6 Z" />

            @* C = (30, 9.5). The vertex the tangent contour touches. *@
            <path class="fc-lp-optimum" d="M 224 223.1 L 233.5 232.6 L 224 242.1 L 214.5 232.6 Z" />

            <g class="fc-lp-axis">
                <path d="M 44 14 V 314 H 464" />
                <path class="fc-lp-tip" d="M 44 7 L 40.5 16 L 47.5 16 Z" />
                <path class="fc-lp-tip" d="M 471 314 L 462 310.5 L 462 317.5 Z" />
                <line x1="164" y1="314" x2="164" y2="320" />
                <line x1="284" y1="314" x2="284" y2="320" />
                <line x1="404" y1="314" x2="404" y2="320" />
                <line x1="44" y1="228.3" x2="38" y2="228.3" />
                <line x1="44" y1="142.6" x2="38" y2="142.6" />
                <line x1="44" y1="56.9" x2="38" y2="56.9" />
            </g>
        </svg>

        <span class="fc-lp-axlabel fc-lp-axlabel-x">Maize %</span>
    </div>

    @* The key is markup rather than part of the drawing, for the same reason. The swatches are the
       same strokes and fills the plot uses, at 12 units tall. Ordered the way the figure is built up
       -- requirements, what they leave, what it costs, which way that falls, where it bottoms out --
       so reading the key top to bottom is the argument in five lines. *@
    <ul class="fc-lp-key">
        <li>
            <svg class="fc-lp-chip" viewBox="0 0 24 12" aria-hidden="true">
                <line class="fc-lp-constraint" x1="1" y1="10" x2="23" y2="2" />
            </svg>
            <span>A nutrient requirement &mdash; protein, energy, lysine, calcium</span>
        </li>
        <li>
            <svg class="fc-lp-chip" viewBox="0 0 24 12" aria-hidden="true">
                <path class="fc-lp-region" d="M 2 10 L 6 2 L 22 3 L 18 10 Z" />
            </svg>
            <span>Every mix that meets all of them</span>
        </li>
        <li>
            <svg class="fc-lp-chip" viewBox="0 0 24 12" aria-hidden="true">
                <line class="fc-lp-iso" x1="1" y1="10" x2="23" y2="2" />
            </svg>
            <span>Mixes that cost the same</span>
        </li>
        <li>
            <svg class="fc-lp-chip" viewBox="0 0 24 12" aria-hidden="true">
                <g class="fc-lp-arrow">
                    <line x1="21" y1="2" x2="9" y2="8.4" />
                    <path d="M 4.5 10.7 L 7.75 6.16 L 10.09 10.58 Z" />
                </g>
            </svg>
            <span>Cost falls this way</span>
        </li>
        <li>
            <svg class="fc-lp-chip" viewBox="0 0 24 12" aria-hidden="true">
                <path class="fc-lp-optimum" d="M 12 1 L 18 6 L 12 11 L 6 6 Z" />
            </svg>
            <span>The cheapest one in it &mdash; what FeedCraft returns</span>
        </li>
    </ul>

    <figcaption class="fc-lp-note">
        Two ingredients and three requirements, so it fits on a page. A real ration has dozens of
        each, and the answer is still a single corner of the same shape.
    </figcaption>
</figure>
